1|0@MensRights^1440682451#cuhf3v1:NerdyWeightLifter$:Actually, it's you failing on probability knowledge. It's a well understood problem with high levels of false positives. Specifically, there is a relatively tiny number of people that end up killing their families in comparison to the size of the population. Even if you could afford to profile the whole population to select just the people that fit this profile, you would still likely have a million of so people to try to help because this is vague social science criteria. Then you'd have to pay to treat this million people over a long period, assuming they actually accept that they needed it, and in the process, creating a stigma for many of them, potentially destabilising relationships and causing harm that would otherwise have never occurred. The whole thing is a nonsense idea. There are without doubt far more certain and lower cost things a government could do to avoid violence in the community. 
