16|0@MensRights^1440650140#cuh50sm:Mitschu$:I can't vouch for the veracity, but the scale is misleading by itself. Two women every week sounds like a lot... until you remember that we're discussing a population of 64 million people, of whom about 550 a year are victims of homicide. That means the general population faces 1.5 homicides a day, of which if we take the "average of two a week" from above for women killed by partners, we get 19% (2 per week (0.286 per day) / 1.5 total per day) of all homicide victims are women slain by partners. So... in a country where the homicide victimization rate is is 0.000859375% (or 1 victim per 116,000 population) per *year*... less than 20% of those 0.0009% of society that are victims are female victims of IPV homicide... or put another way, 0.000171875% (1 per 600,000 population) of all UK citizens are females who will die to domestic abuse. To express it a different way, for every 5 female IPV homicide victims in the UK, someone else in the UK gets struck by a bolt of lightning. (1/600,000 vs 1/3,000,000) And that's being generous and assuming that they didn't flub the numbers any further (than just misrepresenting the scale) to make them higher.
