8|0@MensRights^1440653472#cuh6c5g:Mitschu$:It's one of the older tricks in the book when cooking statistics. Change the scale to something immediately appreciable, instead of accurate. The following are all legitimate statistics, taken from various sources and made vastly more scary: --- Every ten hours, someone dies from falling down stairs in the US. #banstaircases Last year, the number of people who died in their sleep tripled. #bansleeping Every two hours, an infant chokes on a meal. #bansolidfood For that matter, on average 192 people every *week* **including infants** (gotta have that special victim appeal) choke to death. That's more than one preventable death every hour. #banfoodingeneral 1 in 5 drowning victims are children under the age of 14, and a child drowns every twelve hours in the US alone. #banwater --- Hell, I had a boss who loved performing a variant of that trick. He'd point out that on *average* millions of freight cases were thrown up every hour at stores around the globe... so he wasn't asking for much when ordering me to get my case per hour count above 200. (The research I did there indicated that the average case CPH rate globally per worker was 55... but the company had a ***lot*** of employees, worldwide.) As a general rule of thumb, when people are discussing statistics and they use measurements like "X per hour" or "X percent increase" without giving you a measure of context like "X per hour per Y population" or "X percent increase from Y to Z", they're bullshitting you and hoping you don't check and just accept it on a gut level.
